#### 3. two layers, closed form
ok   f1=1->3, f2=1->1: mpl - minimiser at x1 == (f2*d1 - f1*d2)/T = 1/2  J mpl 7/4 vs min 3/4
ok   f1=1->1, f2=1->3: mpl - minimiser at x1 == (f2*d1 - f1*d2)/T = -1/2  J mpl 7/4 vs min 3/4
ok   f1=2->2, f2=1->5: mpl - minimiser at x1 == (f2*d1 - f1*d2)/T = -8/7  J mpl 104/7 vs min 40/7
ok   f1=4->1, f2=1->4: mpl - minimiser at x1 == (f2*d1 - f1*d2)/T = -3  J mpl 45 vs min 0
ok   f1=2->4, f2=1->2: mpl - minimiser at x1 == (f2*d1 - f1*d2)/T = 0  J mpl 3 vs min 3
   so the two agree at a step exactly when f2*d1 == f1*d2, i.e. both layers change in proportion to their thickness
#### 4. cases where matplotlib is right
ok   one layer: matplotlib == minimiser (dg0 = -df/2)
ok   palindromic layer order (L0 == L2): matplotlib == minimiser
ok   all layers proportional to one profile: matplotlib == minimiser
#### 5. random small integer layers (exact)
   400 random cases (2-5 layers, 3-8 points, integers 0..9): matplotlib == minimiser in 0
   J(matplotlib)/J(minimiser) over the others: min 1.023, median 5.235, 90th pct 17.809, max 212.436
   largest max|g0_mpl - g0_min| / max total thickness: 0.964
ok   random cases: matplotlib's J is never below the minimiser's (sanity of the minimiser)  0 below
FAIL random cases: matplotlib == minimiser in every case  0/400
